  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 104 AM MDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nHigh pressure will produce another dry day today with highs for\nour valley communities ranging from the mid 60s to low 70s. We \nare watching a low pressure system off the coast of the Pacific \nNorthwest. This low will be working down the coast, eventually \nstalling off the central California coast. Some moisture from \nthis system will be working into the region Tuesday in the form \nof isolated showers and thunderstorms. Possibly could see \nisolated convection into Wednesday as well although the high res\nmodels haven't resolved that quite yet. \n\nAs we move into Thursday the low will quickly move inland and lift \ntowards our region. This will bring good chances for precipitation \nto our region for Thursday and Friday. The low will move east Friday \nwith residual moisture streaming into the region from the northwest \nalong with much colder temperatures. Snow levels with this event \nlook high, falling to 7 or 8000 feet on Friday. Most of the moisture \nexits the region Friday night when snow levels approach valley \nfloors. In terms of qpf, the mean for this event is sitting at 0.25 \nto 0.50 inches for the Magic Valley with up to an inch for the \ncentral mountains and around an inch or higher for the Snake River \nPlain and eastern highlands.",
